{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And Elohim said.</b> He said to His heart and to His thought, for at that time there were no living beings except HaShem alone. And such is the way of the world: a person expresses from his mouth what he is doing. And so it is written: 'It grieved His heart' and 'He said: I will blot out the human.'" "<b>Let there be light.</b> Before the stretching of the heavens, the light of HaShem filled the entire world. When the heavens were stretched like a curtain, the heavens intervened between the light and the deep — therefore 'and darkness was upon the face of the deep.' Therefore He commanded 'let there be light,' and He brought it first because it is beloved and good for every deed." "<b>Let there be light.</b> Three things were created on the first day: heavens, earth, and light. All three conceived and gave birth to all their offspring, each in its time: the heavens brought forth their offspring on the second day, namely the firmament; the earth on the third day — grasses and trees; light on the fourth day — sun and moon and stars. And a proof for this matter: תוצא הארץ — one would not say תוצא except concerning something already prepared, as in 'go forth from the ark.'" "<b>And there was light.</b> What is written ויהי כן in the other creations is because it is brief language. And so we learned: a person should always review for his students in brief. Had He written ויהי רקיע and ותדשא הארץ, it would have been lengthy in expression; but here He does not lengthen the expression ויהי אור beyond ויהי כן."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:18:34.890451644Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}